{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>What did this people do to you that you have brought a great sin upon it?</b> How many sufferings did you endure so that they tested you, until you brought this sin upon them? — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 32:21). And it is not correct in my eyes, for this sin is among the sins for which one must be killed rather than transgress. Perhaps he said this to him in order to magnify his guilt. But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that it is like \"What have I done? What is my crime, and what is my sin before your father, that he seeks my life?\" (1 Samuel 20:1). That is: what hatred did you have against this people that you caused their destruction and annihilation? And because Aaron was for them a man of reproof and atonement (Shemot Rabbah 3:21), and it was fitting that he should show them compassion and mercy, he said this to him — meaning: you dealt with them like an enemy who desires their harm; they did not rebel or sin against you. Now, it would have been fitting for Moses to accuse him first for his own sin that he himself sinned, and afterward to accuse him for the guilt of the people, and to say: How could you commit this great sin against Elohim, and also cause many to stumble and bring upon them a great sin? But Moses, in his humility, acted with honor toward his elder brother and mentioned to him only the stumbling of the people. And it is also possible that Moses's heart was sincerely trusting in the righteousness of his brother, that his intention was not evil; but for the guilt of the people he held him accountable, for it was fitting for him to reprove them and they stumbled through him. And he replied that they misled him with their words:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:20:09.448762521Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}